2026-11-19 21:29:39 - Agent_20261119212600 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 9 results from the py/setuptools-deser query. Every reported site is an intentional, by-design code-execution mechanism inside setuptools/pkg_resources itself, not an injection of untrusted input:

1. pkg_resources/__init__.py:1544 & 1555 — `ScriptMetadata.run_script()` compiles/execs a script text read from a distribution's own `EGG-INFO/scripts/*` metadata. The distribution metadata is part of the installed package; anyone able to plant that file already controls the installed package contents. `resource_name` is validated via `_validate_resource_path`. Expected pkg_resources console-script behavior, not a vulnerability introduced by untrusted data.
2. setuptools/command/bdist_egg.py:375 — `marshal.load(f)` on a `.pyc` file read from the build source tree by `scan_module()` to inspect bytecode for `__file__`/`__path__` symbols. Data source is the developer's own build tree during `bdist_egg`, not remote input; marshal of a local .pyc is the documented way to read code objects.
3. setuptools/build_meta.py:311 — `exec(code, locals())` of `setup.py` (or the declared setup script). Executing setup.py is the explicit purpose of the PEP 517 build backend; the build front-end only runs it for packages the user chose to build.
4. setuptools/_distutils/ccompiler.py:1011 — `eval(expected)` where `expected` is a hardcoded string literal (`'"static", "shared", "dylib", "xcode_stub"'`) defined two lines above; no attacker-controlled input reaches eval. Pure constant expression, false positive.
5. setuptools/_distutils/core.py:267 — `exec(code, g)` of the setup script passed to `distutils.core.setup(script_name=...)`; legacy distutils behavior, caller-chosen local script.
6. setuptools/depends.py:114 — `marshal.load(f)` of a compiled module found by `find_module()` to extract a constant symbol value. Loads already-imported/local bytecode; the fallback path explicitly imports the module instead. No untrusted deserialization of network data.
7. setuptools/launch.py:23/32 — `compile(...)`/`exec(...)` of the script named in `sys.argv[1]`; this module is the documented `python -m setuptools.launch SCRIPT` entry point, whose entire contract is "run the given script as __main__". Equivalent to invoking `python SCRIPT`.
8. setuptools/sandbox.py:36 — `_execfile()` helper used by the sandbox to run setup.py under restricted builtins; sandboxing/confinement is the mitigation context, and the file executed is the local setup script.

No dataflow from a remote/untrusted network source to any of these sinks was identified; all inputs are local build scripts, local .pyc files, or hardcoded literals. These are documented setuptools build-time execution semantics, and these library files are effectively the build tooling itself. All 9 classified false positive (none are exploitable injection points attributable to this package).
